Yes. Pantheon grants more than “user access” to cognition:

* User: runs available mental programs.
* Root: inspects and changes state, memory, routing, and permissions.
* Architect: creates new operators and interfaces.
* Author: changes the representational language in which the system is constructed.

But the jurisdiction matters:

Root over your cognitive tools does not mean root over other people—or ontological root over reality.

Beyond root is authorship of the coordinate system. Reality still does not have to sign its treaty.

Sharing sins as training data

Failures are unusually valuable because they expose the actual decision boundary:

What I perceived
→ what I predicted
→ what I chose
→ what happened
→ where the model failed
→ what would discriminate better next time

But disclosure needs permissions:

PRIVATE:
Full execution trace
TRUSTED:
Accountable trace with sensitive details minimized
PUBLIC:
Deidentified mechanism, consequence, repair, and lesson

Separate:

\text{Person}\neq\text{action}\neq\text{failure}\neq\text{lesson}

Otherwise people may convert an honest error report into a permanent identity judgment. Dehydrate the experience into a transferable lesson without erasing responsibility or exposing information that serves no learning purpose.

Adult education as recurring kata

Adults do not merely need information once. Skills decay:

M(t)\approx M_0e^{-\lambda t}

The system should predict when competence approaches a threshold and surface a short, enjoyable refresher:

Detect fading skill
→ present scenario before explanation
→ require retrieval
→ apply under simulated pressure
→ give immediate feedback
→ ask learner to teach it back
→ schedule next interval adaptively

Make refreshers feel like:

* three-minute boss fights,
* branching real-world scenarios,
* cooperative missions,
* role-based spacetogas,
* error-spotting games,
* “teach the rookie” challenges,
* and skill seals earned through demonstrated performance.

Fun is not decorative. It reduces entry resistance and increases repetitions. But the seal must be earned by retrieval and application, not merely by clicking through.

Classes versus castes

You braided programming classes, educational classes, and social classes into the same word. ;)

A class becomes functionally caste-like when:

* assignment is inherited,
* exit becomes nearly impossible,
* rights depend on membership,
* the label consumes the person’s whole identity,
* and those above it face no accountability.

Mathematically:

P(C_{\text{child}}=C_{\text{parent}})\rightarrow1

P(C_{t+1}\neq C_t\mid competence,\ effort)\rightarrow0

The word class does not rescue a system with caste mechanics. Judge the transition rules, permissions, dignity, and permeability—not the rose’s name.

Functional classes remain legitimate when authority is:

\text{scoped}
+\text{earned}
+\text{accountable}
+\text{reviewable}
+\text{revocable}

And always:

\boxed{\text{Capability hierarchy}\neq\text{human-worth hierarchy}}

Respect as a constitutional invariant

Respect does not mean agreement, approval, surrender, or inaction.

Respect(Person)=\text{constant}

Approval(Action)=\text{conditional}

Permission(Action)=\text{bounded}

We can oppose conduct, enforce boundaries, deny requests, apply consequences, compete intensely, and pursue our aims without degrading anyone’s fundamental worth.

That makes respect structural rather than cosmetic:

Treat people as sovereign persons while still doing what reality, duty, and the mission require.

So an author-level cognitive OS needs exactly what you just named:

Root capability
+ honest failure logs
+ recurring practice
+ mobility between functional classes
+ invariant human dignity

Power without those becomes caste. With them, it becomes disciplined agency.